WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.308 --> 00:00:02.804
- You're not going to
increase your revenue,

00:00:02.804 --> 00:00:05.268
your traffic, your sales

00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:08.317
unless you take growth seriously.

00:00:08.317 --> 00:00:09.291
Perfection is overrated.

00:00:09.291 --> 00:00:10.373
Nothing's ever perfect.

00:00:10.373 --> 00:00:11.537
You're going to continue
to have to iterate,

00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:12.523
so forth, so on.

00:00:12.523 --> 00:00:14.901
Why would you expect someone
to just come to your website

00:00:14.901 --> 00:00:18.256
and be like hey, buy something for $1,000.

00:00:18.256 --> 00:00:20.956
Okay, here's my credit card, right?

00:00:20.956 --> 00:00:22.749
- He's an entrepreneur, investor,

00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:24.345
and analytics expert.

00:00:24.345 --> 00:00:25.902
He's best known as the co-founder

00:00:25.902 --> 00:00:28.209
of KISSmetrics and Crazy Egg.

00:00:28.209 --> 00:00:29.505
In addition to founding companies,

00:00:29.505 --> 00:00:31.975
he's well-regarded as
a writer and a blogger.

00:00:31.975 --> 00:00:33.663
He's Neil Patel, and here's my take

00:00:33.663 --> 00:00:35.762
on his top ten rules for success.

00:00:35.762 --> 00:00:37.111
Rule number three is my personal favorite,

00:00:37.111 --> 00:00:38.193
and I'm curious to figure out

00:00:38.193 --> 00:00:39.881
which one you guys like the best.

00:00:39.881 --> 00:00:40.962
Also, as Neil is talking,

00:00:40.962 --> 00:00:43.021
if he says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:43.021 --> 00:00:44.622
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:44.622 --> 00:00:46.238
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:46.238 --> 00:00:47.964
can be inspired as well.

00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:49.030
Enjoy.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.034
(air rushing)

00:00:52.034 --> 00:00:55.784
(uplifting orchestral music)

00:01:01.997 --> 00:01:02.906
- Before we get started,

00:01:02.906 --> 00:01:06.133
I want to tell you one quote
from a guy named Adam Nash,

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:08.381
and it's very important, in which

00:01:08.381 --> 00:01:09.596
"Growth is important,

00:01:09.596 --> 00:01:13.179
"and all good companies
take it seriously."

00:01:14.182 --> 00:01:16.731
You're not going to increase your revenue,

00:01:16.731 --> 00:01:19.260
your traffic, your sales

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.659
unless you take growth seriously.

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:25.064
For example, you know Facebook.

00:01:25.064 --> 00:01:26.501
You know what their internal metric

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:29.381
when they first started out was?

00:01:29.381 --> 00:01:31.966
How many more users were
they adding per day?

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:34.813
And they weren't just looking
at it as a pure number.

00:01:34.813 --> 00:01:37.739
So if they had 1,000 users the first day

00:01:37.739 --> 00:01:41.181
and the second day they
had another 1,000 users

00:01:41.181 --> 00:01:44.425
and then the third day they
had another new 1,000 users,

00:01:44.425 --> 00:01:45.751
that's slowing growth.

00:01:45.751 --> 00:01:48.327
Percentage-wise, you're
not growing as fast.

00:01:48.327 --> 00:01:51.583
So their goal was to grow a faster percent

00:01:51.583 --> 00:01:53.861
than the previous day, right?

00:01:53.861 --> 00:01:55.792
If you take growth seriously,

00:01:55.792 --> 00:01:57.872
you can actually do quite well.

00:01:57.872 --> 00:01:59.435
You need to execute,

00:01:59.435 --> 00:02:03.234
and you need to execute
as fast as possible.

00:02:03.234 --> 00:02:04.793
Because the thing at the end of the day is

00:02:04.793 --> 00:02:07.765
whether you feel you
have competitors or not,

00:02:07.765 --> 00:02:09.803
sooner or later you're
going to have someone,

00:02:09.803 --> 00:02:11.612
and if you can't move fast enough,

00:02:11.612 --> 00:02:13.688
someone's going to beat you to the punch.

00:02:13.688 --> 00:02:15.283
So make sure whatever you're doing,

00:02:15.283 --> 00:02:17.282
you get something out
within a month or two.

00:02:17.282 --> 00:02:18.621
If it takes you longer than that,

00:02:18.621 --> 00:02:21.246
you're taking way too long.

00:02:21.246 --> 00:02:24.408
I created this company called Serph,

00:02:24.408 --> 00:02:26.549
and it was in Rockwell, Texas,

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:27.845
right by Dallas.

00:02:27.845 --> 00:02:29.952
And I had this brilliant idea.

00:02:29.952 --> 00:02:31.181
Have any of you guys heard of

00:02:31.181 --> 00:02:34.487
the Media Temple grid server solution?

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:37.853
That was my idea a year
or two before it came out.

00:02:37.853 --> 00:02:40.244
And I spent a million bucks on it

00:02:40.244 --> 00:02:43.029
trying to actually do that
idea before Media Temple.

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:44.935
The funny thing is I didn't
have a million bucks,

00:02:44.935 --> 00:02:47.221
so I actually had to
borrow a million bucks,

00:02:47.221 --> 00:02:51.068
and I spent it on business
partners who moved really slow

00:02:51.068 --> 00:02:53.504
and really weren't the
best at what they did.

00:02:53.504 --> 00:02:56.455
Within 12 months, we launched nothing.

00:02:56.455 --> 00:02:57.672
They also had some of the problems

00:02:57.672 --> 00:02:59.404
in my previous slide, right,

00:02:59.404 --> 00:03:01.826
like financial instability

00:03:01.826 --> 00:03:04.698
and they had a family and
kids of I think, like, six.

00:03:04.698 --> 00:03:06.458
So I even bought 'em a
home in Rockwell, Texas

00:03:06.458 --> 00:03:08.989
that cost 200 and something thousand.

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:10.816
Why did I buy it instead of renting it?

00:03:10.816 --> 00:03:11.660
I have no clue.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.965
I was stupid and young.

00:03:13.965 --> 00:03:17.949
But nonetheless, I lost
over a million bucks

00:03:17.949 --> 00:03:19.177
on that of borrowed money.

00:03:19.177 --> 00:03:21.054
Not investor money, but borrowed money

00:03:21.054 --> 00:03:23.770
that I had to pay back.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:27.176
And it failed miserably because
we never launched anything.

00:03:27.176 --> 00:03:28.535
If you can't launch something,

00:03:28.535 --> 00:03:30.710
someone like Media Temple's
going to beat you to the bunch,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:31.600
get a ton of customers,

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.258
and make a lot of money.

00:03:33.258 --> 00:03:35.091
So lessons I learned from that.

00:03:35.091 --> 00:03:36.611
Set deadlines.

00:03:36.611 --> 00:03:39.361
No matter what, you have to set deadlines.

00:03:39.361 --> 00:03:42.150
People have to be held
accountable to 'em as well, right?

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.215
You can't just set a deadline,

00:03:43.215 --> 00:03:45.382
it passes, nothing happens.

00:03:45.382 --> 00:03:47.114
Someone better be meeting 'em.

00:03:47.114 --> 00:03:49.562
If not, you know, something has to change.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.667
You also need to make sure they're small.

00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:54.525
If the deadline, if the milestones, goals

00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:56.777
are too big for those deadlines,

00:03:56.777 --> 00:03:58.134
they're not going to get met,

00:03:58.134 --> 00:04:00.851
so make 'em really small and tangible.

00:04:00.851 --> 00:04:04.146
That way you can see it
really well in advance

00:04:04.146 --> 00:04:06.786
if they're actually
going to be met or not.

00:04:06.786 --> 00:04:08.322
And last but not least,

00:04:08.322 --> 00:04:09.633
don't worry about perfection.

00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:10.649
A lot of people, like,

00:04:10.649 --> 00:04:14.166
dude, I'm going to create
the most perfect version of,

00:04:14.166 --> 00:04:15.777
you know, whatever it may be.

00:04:15.777 --> 00:04:16.941
That software has a lot of buzz.

00:04:16.941 --> 00:04:18.330
Mine's going to be perfect.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:19.458
Perfection is overrated.

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:20.516
Nothing's ever perfect.

00:04:20.516 --> 00:04:21.825
You're going to continue
to have to iterate

00:04:21.825 --> 00:04:22.967
so forth, so on.

00:04:22.967 --> 00:04:24.359
It's never going to be perfect.

00:04:24.359 --> 00:04:25.638
There's always going to be issues.

00:04:25.638 --> 00:04:28.354
Just get it out there and
start trying to make money.

00:04:28.354 --> 00:04:30.630
I continually learn from my mistakes

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.569
and other people's mistakes.

00:04:32.569 --> 00:04:34.566
See, entrepreneurs think that,

00:04:34.566 --> 00:04:36.275
hey, to create that billion-dollar company

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:37.294
or hundred-million-dollar company

00:04:37.294 --> 00:04:40.303
or even million-dollar company
you have to be really smart,

00:04:40.303 --> 00:04:42.556
and sure, brains is part of it,

00:04:42.556 --> 00:04:44.715
luck is a huge component.

00:04:44.715 --> 00:04:46.081
But what I've learned over the years

00:04:46.081 --> 00:04:49.209
is everyone, no matter how smart you are,

00:04:49.209 --> 00:04:52.078
you're going to fail many times
and you're going to make mistakes.

00:04:52.078 --> 00:04:53.350
Just look at Mark Zuckerberg.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.047
Facebook worked out.

00:04:55.047 --> 00:04:57.299
His version of Snapchat that was a copycat

00:04:57.299 --> 00:04:59.352
did not work out, right?

00:04:59.352 --> 00:05:02.418
So there's other areas
in which Facebook failed,

00:05:02.418 --> 00:05:05.200
but Facebook overall as
a company has done great.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.176
Just because you're smart doesn't mean

00:05:06.176 --> 00:05:07.331
you're always going to succeed

00:05:07.331 --> 00:05:09.672
with everything you try or touch.

00:05:09.672 --> 00:05:12.303
But every time you do something,

00:05:12.303 --> 00:05:13.710
you will learn new things,

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.093
you will make mistakes,

00:05:15.093 --> 00:05:16.635
and if you learn from these mistakes

00:05:16.635 --> 00:05:19.871
and avoid making 'em over and over again,

00:05:19.871 --> 00:05:21.436
you'll actually increase your odds

00:05:21.436 --> 00:05:23.447
of succeeding as time goes on.

00:05:23.447 --> 00:05:24.515
And the best way to do this

00:05:24.515 --> 00:05:26.500
is not just to learn from your mistakes,

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.106
but it's also to learn from
the mistakes of others.

00:05:29.106 --> 00:05:31.519
Rockefeller, Carnegie, all these people

00:05:31.519 --> 00:05:35.780
who were entrepreneurs years
ago in the 1800s, 1900s,

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:39.075
they themselves used business
principles and concepts

00:05:39.075 --> 00:05:41.890
that are still applicable
in today's world.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:42.982
Sure, we have the internet,

00:05:42.982 --> 00:05:45.330
but the same strategies
and concepts that they used

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.999
are still applicable, right?

00:05:46.999 --> 00:05:48.353
So learn from their mistakes.

00:05:48.353 --> 00:05:50.404
Learn what worked for them
and what didn't work them,

00:05:50.404 --> 00:05:54.162
why, and bring that into
what you're doing today.

00:05:54.162 --> 00:05:58.576
So conversion optimization
is like dating, right?

00:05:58.576 --> 00:06:02.155
So if I go up to a random person,

00:06:02.155 --> 00:06:04.121
let's say the lady looking
down on her laptop,

00:06:04.121 --> 00:06:07.255
anyone, Unbound shirt, what's your name?

00:06:07.255 --> 00:06:08.351
What's your name?

00:06:08.351 --> 00:06:09.275
- [Nadia] Nadia.

00:06:09.275 --> 00:06:12.229
- Nadia, if I say will
you marry me right now,

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:14.894
chances are you're going to say?

00:06:14.894 --> 00:06:16.209
All right, if she was single,

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:17.971
she would still probably say no.

00:06:17.971 --> 00:06:19.640
Why would she say no?

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.708
(laughing)

00:06:22.708 --> 00:06:24.581
Yes, she is smart,

00:06:24.581 --> 00:06:27.177
but what's the other reason?

00:06:27.177 --> 00:06:28.621
- [Audience Member] Not enough data.

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:30.631
- Okay, not enough data, but what else?

00:06:30.631 --> 00:06:32.889
If someone comes up to you
in the middle of the street

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:35.004
and just says, will you marry me,

00:06:35.004 --> 00:06:35.956
I don't care if they look,

00:06:35.956 --> 00:06:38.049
they're a Victoria's Secret model, right?

00:06:38.049 --> 00:06:39.412
Why would you say no,

00:06:39.412 --> 00:06:40.651
or I don't care if they're Brad Pitt.

00:06:40.651 --> 00:06:41.638
- [Audience Member] I don't know you.

00:06:41.638 --> 00:06:43.571
- Exactly, you don't know 'em.

00:06:43.571 --> 00:06:44.404
That's right.

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:47.089
Whoever said, give
yourself a pat on the back.

00:06:47.089 --> 00:06:49.482
That's the way the internet works, too.

00:06:49.482 --> 00:06:51.757
Why would you expect someone
to just come to your website

00:06:51.757 --> 00:06:55.122
and be like, hey, buy
something for $1,000.

00:06:55.122 --> 00:06:57.797
Okay, here's my credit card, right?

00:06:57.797 --> 00:06:59.482
I wish the world worked like that,

00:06:59.482 --> 00:07:01.109
but it doesn't.

00:07:01.109 --> 00:07:02.827
But once you get to know people,

00:07:02.827 --> 00:07:04.947
they're much more likely
to say yes, right?

00:07:04.947 --> 00:07:06.279
Just like you date someone,

00:07:06.279 --> 00:07:08.129
get to know 'em, move in together,

00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:09.492
you ask 'em to marry you.

00:07:09.492 --> 00:07:10.527
Hopefully they say yes.

00:07:10.527 --> 00:07:13.867
If not, you kind of wasted some time.

00:07:13.867 --> 00:07:15.821
But here's what most funnels look like.

00:07:15.821 --> 00:07:18.730
Someone comes to your website, sign up,

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:21.585
and you generate some sales, right?

00:07:21.585 --> 00:07:23.780
So I decided to do something.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:24.780
I'm like, you know what?

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:26.865
Why not flip around the funnel?

00:07:26.865 --> 00:07:30.105
Why not get people to
actually use a product

00:07:30.105 --> 00:07:31.650
before I actually try to charge 'em?

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:33.210
Put in a URL first.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.118
From there, what's your goal?

00:07:35.118 --> 00:07:37.554
Start creating product, right?

00:07:37.554 --> 00:07:42.017
It actually caused a
52.11% increase in signups,

00:07:42.017 --> 00:07:43.657
all by switching it around.

00:07:43.657 --> 00:07:45.596
Still asked for their credit card.

00:07:45.596 --> 00:07:47.114
Just got more signups.

00:07:47.114 --> 00:07:48.845
Why, because people got to play around

00:07:48.845 --> 00:07:52.171
with the product a bit
before they actually bought.

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:53.838
Focus, focus, focus.

00:07:54.686 --> 00:07:58.200
You heard the say, us Generation
Y kids have ADD, right?

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.572
That's what we all say.

00:07:59.572 --> 00:08:02.787
Well, if you have ADD, you know,

00:08:02.787 --> 00:08:04.868
you got to figure out how to focus,

00:08:04.868 --> 00:08:06.655
and here's the thing.

00:08:06.655 --> 00:08:10.162
There isn't enough time in
the day to do multiple things.

00:08:10.162 --> 00:08:11.586
Think of it this way.

00:08:11.586 --> 00:08:13.041
I'm a single guy.

00:08:13.041 --> 00:08:13.958
I'm 27 now.

00:08:15.278 --> 00:08:17.164
And if you think about it,

00:08:17.164 --> 00:08:18.490
there's only 24 hours in a day,

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:20.028
seven days a week.

00:08:20.028 --> 00:08:20.944
Let's say you sleep.

00:08:20.944 --> 00:08:24.205
I need my eight hours
of sleep or I'm cranky.

00:08:24.205 --> 00:08:26.606
It's like, you know, you
hang out with framily,

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:27.892
whatever, maybe, you don't really have

00:08:27.892 --> 00:08:31.374
more than 70 to 80 hours
to work every single week.

00:08:31.374 --> 00:08:33.814
If you barely have money
and there's not enough

00:08:33.814 --> 00:08:35.834
time in the day to work, right,

00:08:35.834 --> 00:08:38.024
to get everything done
that needs to be done,

00:08:38.024 --> 00:08:39.928
how can you do multiple businesses

00:08:39.928 --> 00:08:43.211
and do two businesses at the same time?

00:08:43.211 --> 00:08:44.616
It's just not going to work.

00:08:44.616 --> 00:08:46.164
Unless you're Elon Musk,

00:08:46.164 --> 00:08:48.184
and I got to say, that
guy's a badass, right?

00:08:48.184 --> 00:08:49.017
Like, when people ask him,

00:08:49.017 --> 00:08:49.850
what do you do,

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:53.237
he can say, "Oh, one of my
companies competes with Toyota."

00:08:53.237 --> 00:08:54.471
What's the other one do?

00:08:54.471 --> 00:08:56.195
"I compete with NASA," right?

00:08:56.195 --> 00:08:57.383
Like, he's a badass,

00:08:57.383 --> 00:08:59.251
and he's the CEO of both of 'em.

00:08:59.251 --> 00:09:00.813
And they're billion-dollar companies.

00:09:00.813 --> 00:09:02.301
Unless you're Elon Musk,

00:09:02.301 --> 00:09:04.800
don't try to do two
companies at once, right?

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:06.950
He put in, when people
didn't give him money,

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:08.553
in one interview, he's like,

00:09:08.553 --> 00:09:09.657
"No one would give me money.

00:09:09.657 --> 00:09:12.202
"I just put in 100 million
bucks of my own money," right?

00:09:12.202 --> 00:09:13.934
Like, the guy's really a badass,

00:09:13.934 --> 00:09:16.888
and probably none of us
in this room are him,

00:09:16.888 --> 00:09:19.866
so don't try to do two businesses at once.

00:09:19.866 --> 00:09:20.996
Focus.

00:09:20.996 --> 00:09:22.822
Idea number six.

00:09:22.822 --> 00:09:24.399
Get personal.

00:09:24.399 --> 00:09:27.189
And I got this idea
from Facebook years ago.

00:09:27.189 --> 00:09:28.539
Do you remember when you were on Facebook,

00:09:28.539 --> 00:09:32.621
like, five years ago and
you would see all these ads,

00:09:32.621 --> 00:09:34.459
your friend has a crush on you?

00:09:34.459 --> 00:09:35.833
You remember that?

00:09:35.833 --> 00:09:36.898
Those guys made a killing.

00:09:36.898 --> 00:09:38.678
They were spammers, but
they made a killing.

00:09:38.678 --> 00:09:40.370
And I'm not saying you should spam,

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:43.363
but you can actually learn a
lot from these kind of people.

00:09:43.363 --> 00:09:44.915
Don't do the shady stuff they're doing,

00:09:44.915 --> 00:09:47.379
but take the ethical
tactics that they were doing

00:09:47.379 --> 00:09:48.937
and replicate them and throw out

00:09:48.937 --> 00:09:51.213
the shady business model part.

00:09:51.213 --> 00:09:54.654
So dating sites make you feel special,

00:09:54.654 --> 00:09:56.321
very special, right?

00:09:57.665 --> 00:10:00.162
They tell you when you go to these sites,

00:10:00.162 --> 00:10:01.745
OkCupid, Match.com,

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:05.920
what else is there?

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.128
Tinder doesn't do it,

00:10:07.128 --> 00:10:08.371
'cause Tinder's mobile-based.

00:10:08.371 --> 00:10:09.684
Actually, Tinder kind of does it.

00:10:09.684 --> 00:10:11.259
It's geo-based, right?

00:10:11.259 --> 00:10:12.846
They make you feel special.

00:10:12.846 --> 00:10:15.677
So if I'm logging in from
Vancouver and I go to Match.

00:10:15.677 --> 00:10:16.892
I don't know if Match is here.

00:10:16.892 --> 00:10:18.801
It's an IC company.

00:10:18.801 --> 00:10:22.854
It usually says, hey, there's, you know,

00:10:22.854 --> 00:10:25.104
5,639 singles in Vancouver.

00:10:27.667 --> 00:10:29.440
What are you waiting for, right?

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.783
It's like, oh, that's a lot
of single people in Vancouver.

00:10:31.783 --> 00:10:34.042
Maybe I should sign up.

00:10:34.042 --> 00:10:37.209
So we tested this out on timsykes.com.

00:10:38.224 --> 00:10:41.853
I'm determined to create a
millionaire trader in Las Vegas.

00:10:41.853 --> 00:10:45.353
My only question is will it be you, right?

00:10:46.388 --> 00:10:48.375
Tim is down to create
a millionaire trader.

00:10:48.375 --> 00:10:49.797
He's done this many times.

00:10:49.797 --> 00:10:52.464
We started using geo-based data.

00:10:53.323 --> 00:10:56.760
The signup rate actually
increased quite a bit.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.582
We decided to do the
same thing on my site.

00:10:59.582 --> 00:11:00.822
Do you want more traffic?

00:11:00.822 --> 00:11:02.346
Hey, I'm Neil Patel.

00:11:02.346 --> 00:11:06.277
I'm determined to make a
business in Las Vegas successful.

00:11:06.277 --> 00:11:08.882
My only question is will it be you?

00:11:08.882 --> 00:11:10.970
The best part about this is

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.761
for all the people around Las
Vegas or cities like that,

00:11:14.761 --> 00:11:16.711
geo-targeting's not exact,

00:11:16.711 --> 00:11:18.763
so it may say Las Vegas
even when someone's

00:11:18.763 --> 00:11:20.713
like 30, 40 miles away.

00:11:20.713 --> 00:11:22.741
You know how many emails
we were getting per day?

00:11:22.741 --> 00:11:24.058
Like, hey, I applied.

00:11:24.058 --> 00:11:24.891
I really want to do it.

00:11:24.891 --> 00:11:26.239
I'm not based in Las Vegas,

00:11:26.239 --> 00:11:27.692
but I'm only 20 miles away.

00:11:27.692 --> 00:11:28.525
I can drive.

00:11:28.525 --> 00:11:31.309
Traffic's not too bad, right?

00:11:31.309 --> 00:11:32.465
People were really engaged,

00:11:32.465 --> 00:11:33.743
like, wow, this is personal.

00:11:33.743 --> 00:11:37.877
It's just an opportunity for
someone who's in Las Vegas.

00:11:37.877 --> 00:11:39.917
Heck, he doesn't service and nor do I,

00:11:39.917 --> 00:11:41.741
we don't service people
in Hamburg, Germany,

00:11:41.741 --> 00:11:42.620
but if you go to the page,

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:43.869
it'll show up Hamburg, Germany

00:11:43.869 --> 00:11:45.803
if you're in Hamburg, Germany, right?

00:11:45.803 --> 00:11:47.731
That's what we do.

00:11:47.731 --> 00:11:50.738
Then we also took this data
and decided to throw it,

00:11:50.738 --> 00:11:52.908
throw out all the steps from geo,

00:11:52.908 --> 00:11:54.272
popping in the name,

00:11:54.272 --> 00:11:57.330
changing it to multiple-step
process instead of single,

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:58.869
so that way people are
actually feeling special

00:11:58.869 --> 00:12:01.433
throughout the whole signup process.

00:12:01.433 --> 00:12:02.522
You know what happened?

00:12:02.522 --> 00:12:04.906
68% increase in engagements.

00:12:04.906 --> 00:12:08.530
So conversions jump roughly
30% from signing up,

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:09.877
but engagement was more important.

00:12:09.877 --> 00:12:10.863
What I mean by engagement,

00:12:10.863 --> 00:12:13.086
I'm talking about LTV of the customer.

00:12:13.086 --> 00:12:16.506
Customers were staying 68% longer,

00:12:16.506 --> 00:12:19.779
and I know that may not
sound like a big deal, right,

00:12:19.779 --> 00:12:21.690
but it's really hard to keep customers.

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:23.861
If your customers are
only staying for a year

00:12:23.861 --> 00:12:26.679
and you're getting 'em to
stay for an extra, like,

00:12:26.679 --> 00:12:29.839
seven months, that's huge, right?

00:12:29.839 --> 00:12:33.313
That's seven months' worth of extra money.

00:12:33.313 --> 00:12:34.983
So personalizing a site,

00:12:34.983 --> 00:12:38.493
making people feel special like
they do on the dating sites

00:12:38.493 --> 00:12:40.601
can really help you grow your revenue.

00:12:40.601 --> 00:12:42.171
I really love what I'm doing.

00:12:42.171 --> 00:12:43.860
Not just so much where people are like,

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.868
oh, find your passion and go do it.

00:12:45.868 --> 00:12:49.397
A lot of people claim it's
something they really like.

00:12:49.397 --> 00:12:52.026
But I enjoy marketing so much

00:12:52.026 --> 00:12:54.488
and creating companies for marketers

00:12:54.488 --> 00:12:55.589
that I work my butt off

00:12:55.589 --> 00:12:57.306
and it doesn't seem like work, right?

00:12:57.306 --> 00:13:00.396
I can work 70, 80-hour weeks consistently

00:13:00.396 --> 00:13:01.558
and not have to worry about it.

00:13:01.558 --> 00:13:02.811
It never stresses me out.

00:13:02.811 --> 00:13:03.940
I never feel like, oh,

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.433
I should take a vacation or any of that.

00:13:05.433 --> 00:13:06.748
I really do enjoy working.

00:13:06.748 --> 00:13:08.046
I'm a workaholic.

00:13:08.046 --> 00:13:09.529
So loyalty matters.

00:13:09.529 --> 00:13:11.453
Hiring good employees isn't enough.

00:13:11.453 --> 00:13:13.065
You need to hire loyal employees

00:13:13.065 --> 00:13:16.791
because shit happens that's
just not in your control, right?

00:13:16.791 --> 00:13:18.745
I was part of this
company called Fruitcast.

00:13:18.745 --> 00:13:21.215
I put in 100 grand, me and my co-founder,

00:13:21.215 --> 00:13:24.144
and this dude came up to us with the idea.

00:13:24.144 --> 00:13:25.086
We set up a business,

00:13:25.086 --> 00:13:26.231
we barely did any work,

00:13:26.231 --> 00:13:28.071
we just put in more of the capital.

00:13:28.071 --> 00:13:30.339
It was when podcasting was hot.

00:13:30.339 --> 00:13:32.126
Everyone remember podcasting,

00:13:32.126 --> 00:13:34.026
that thing that no one listens to anymore?

00:13:34.026 --> 00:13:36.096
So when podcasting was hot

00:13:36.096 --> 00:13:40.051
and you just had the iPod
without the video, right,

00:13:40.051 --> 00:13:43.200
we created this company
kind of like Google Adwords,

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:45.930
but for podcast, 'cause it was taking off.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.976
We could automatically take your podcast

00:13:47.976 --> 00:13:50.676
that you were creating
and put in ads for you

00:13:50.676 --> 00:13:54.066
and do on-demand pay-per-listen model,

00:13:54.066 --> 00:13:55.683
and it was hot, right?

00:13:55.683 --> 00:13:57.953
A lot of companies were
hitting us up, ad agencies.

00:13:57.953 --> 00:14:00.824
We even had a lot of buyout offers.

00:14:00.824 --> 00:14:03.342
And it didn't go through.

00:14:03.342 --> 00:14:06.441
The reason the buyout
offer didn't go through,

00:14:06.441 --> 00:14:08.803
and it was from a big search giant,

00:14:08.803 --> 00:14:11.509
you can take a guess, there's only three,

00:14:11.509 --> 00:14:13.215
but the reason it didn't go through,

00:14:13.215 --> 00:14:14.231
'cause he was like, dude,

00:14:14.231 --> 00:14:15.740
why would I want to work for 'em?

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:16.995
That would be stupid.

00:14:16.995 --> 00:14:18.497
I like doing my own little business.

00:14:18.497 --> 00:14:19.485
And I'm like, dude,

00:14:19.485 --> 00:14:21.200
I just gave you 100
grand for this business

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:22.915
and you're telling me you're
not passionate about it

00:14:22.915 --> 00:14:24.195
and you don't want to do it full time

00:14:24.195 --> 00:14:25.531
or you don't care for it?

00:14:25.531 --> 00:14:28.100
I'm like, why'd I give you
the 100 grand for it, right?

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.283
So I learned that it's really important

00:14:30.283 --> 00:14:31.896
to have passionate people.

00:14:31.896 --> 00:14:33.664
Like, the other thing to keep in mind is,

00:14:33.664 --> 00:14:34.973
I've also been through lawsuits,

00:14:34.973 --> 00:14:36.866
class action lawsuits.

00:14:36.866 --> 00:14:38.556
When you don't have loyal people

00:14:38.556 --> 00:14:40.297
that don't fit in with your culture,

00:14:40.297 --> 00:14:41.350
they're going to leave you.

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:43.071
During my last class action lawsuit,

00:14:43.071 --> 00:14:46.077
my last and hopefully only one,

00:14:46.077 --> 00:14:48.718
we actually had no employees who left us,

00:14:48.718 --> 00:14:50.149
which is pretty amazing, right?

00:14:50.149 --> 00:14:52.017
You're running out of capital,

00:14:52.017 --> 00:14:53.804
customers were leaving us,

00:14:53.804 --> 00:14:56.795
and the class action lasted a long time,

00:14:56.795 --> 00:14:57.940
like over a year,

00:14:57.940 --> 00:14:59.687
and no one left us,

00:14:59.687 --> 00:15:01.777
and it was because they
really fit in with culture.

00:15:01.777 --> 00:15:02.951
We took care of 'em.

00:15:02.951 --> 00:15:05.366
Me and my co-founders do whatever we can

00:15:05.366 --> 00:15:07.109
for our team members, right,

00:15:07.109 --> 00:15:08.844
'cause it's like, they're a family.

00:15:08.844 --> 00:15:10.561
So it's like, when you treat 'em that way,

00:15:10.561 --> 00:15:12.021
they're going to treat you that way,

00:15:12.021 --> 00:15:13.791
and it's a win-win situation.

00:15:13.791 --> 00:15:15.351
So lessons learned.

00:15:15.351 --> 00:15:17.311
Hire people who fit within your culture.

00:15:17.311 --> 00:15:18.972
Tony Hsieh does a great job of this.

00:15:18.972 --> 00:15:20.087
When you're interviewing,

00:15:20.087 --> 00:15:21.474
he'll pay you to quit right away,

00:15:21.474 --> 00:15:22.701
and if you want to quit
and take the money,

00:15:22.701 --> 00:15:23.697
you can take the money,

00:15:23.697 --> 00:15:25.803
but he knows that you're a bad fit.

00:15:25.803 --> 00:15:27.974
Make sure everyone has similar short-term

00:15:27.974 --> 00:15:28.807
and long-term models.

00:15:28.807 --> 00:15:30.560
People try to buy you out.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:31.627
If everyone on the team doesn't have

00:15:31.627 --> 00:15:33.095
that same goal and objective,

00:15:33.095 --> 00:15:34.727
a lot of your team members won't go,

00:15:34.727 --> 00:15:35.995
which means that your company's

00:15:35.995 --> 00:15:37.576
not going to be valued the same.

00:15:37.576 --> 00:15:39.183
You also need to be transparent.

00:15:39.183 --> 00:15:41.161
I've always been open with my team,

00:15:41.161 --> 00:15:42.421
and by being transparent,

00:15:42.421 --> 00:15:44.828
it's helped create a good
culture and a healthy one

00:15:44.828 --> 00:15:48.081
where they stick with me and
I continually stick with them.

00:15:48.081 --> 00:15:49.154
You have to keep in mind,

00:15:49.154 --> 00:15:51.244
humans are inherently lazy.

00:15:51.244 --> 00:15:53.959
When you're blogging, you
need to be consistent.

00:15:53.959 --> 00:15:55.678
If you're going to write a post,

00:15:55.678 --> 00:15:57.009
write continually.

00:15:57.009 --> 00:15:58.260
If you're going to write once a week,

00:15:58.260 --> 00:15:59.143
you better write once a week.

00:15:59.143 --> 00:16:00.550
I don't care if it's a holiday.

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:01.883
I don't care if you're sick.

00:16:01.883 --> 00:16:02.841
If you're going to do once a month,

00:16:02.841 --> 00:16:04.526
then stick to once a month.

00:16:04.526 --> 00:16:07.268
Worst case, you should
stick to once a month,

00:16:07.268 --> 00:16:08.310
and then if you have more time,

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:12.316
ramp it up to twice a month
and once a week, et cetera.

00:16:12.316 --> 00:16:14.686
I got lazy one time with my current blog,

00:16:14.686 --> 00:16:16.180
Quick Sprout, years ago,

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:18.496
and I took a whole month off.

00:16:18.496 --> 00:16:20.274
My traffic was climbing
almost every single month

00:16:20.274 --> 00:16:21.441
prior to that.

00:16:22.571 --> 00:16:24.626
When I took a month off,

00:16:24.626 --> 00:16:26.221
how many months do you think it took

00:16:26.221 --> 00:16:28.846
for me to recover that traffic?

00:16:28.846 --> 00:16:30.403
Three.

00:16:30.403 --> 00:16:32.640
I had to blog consistently every week

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:35.098
for three months just
to recuperate my traffic

00:16:35.098 --> 00:16:36.592
back to where it was

00:16:36.592 --> 00:16:39.151
versus it growing on a consistent basis.

00:16:39.151 --> 00:16:41.806
Now sure, the blog won't ever go to zero,

00:16:41.806 --> 00:16:43.214
but it does go down,

00:16:43.214 --> 00:16:45.303
so by continually adding more information,

00:16:45.303 --> 00:16:47.661
you're going to find growth.

00:16:47.661 --> 00:16:48.999
When you're also doing this,

00:16:48.999 --> 00:16:51.096
you need to create a conversation.

00:16:51.096 --> 00:16:52.347
Well, in blogging,

00:16:52.347 --> 00:16:54.803
you can use the words you and I.

00:16:54.803 --> 00:16:56.222
It helps create a conversation, right,

00:16:56.222 --> 00:16:57.429
creates that illusion.

00:16:57.429 --> 00:16:58.743
So if you read my content on Quick Sprout,

00:16:58.743 --> 00:17:00.606
you'll notice a lot of yous and Is.

00:17:00.606 --> 00:17:02.097
Helps keep people engaged,

00:17:02.097 --> 00:17:03.965
makes 'em scroll longer, stay on the page,

00:17:03.965 --> 00:17:06.281
et cetera, comment, right?

00:17:06.281 --> 00:17:08.567
In essence, I'm creating
a virtual conversation,

00:17:08.567 --> 00:17:10.257
and I kid you not, it works.

00:17:10.257 --> 00:17:14.506
The other thing is don't
act smart when you blog.

00:17:14.506 --> 00:17:15.589
Dumb it down.

00:17:16.467 --> 00:17:18.112
So not only should you
create a conversation

00:17:18.112 --> 00:17:21.001
by using you and I, keep it simple.

00:17:21.001 --> 00:17:22.827
Don't try to be more sophisticated

00:17:22.827 --> 00:17:24.283
or smarter than your readers.

00:17:24.283 --> 00:17:26.443
You need them as much as they need you.

00:17:26.443 --> 00:17:28.892
You actually may even
need 'em more, right?

00:17:28.892 --> 00:17:31.551
So be friendly with them.

00:17:31.551 --> 00:17:34.157
Once you got the conversation piece down,

00:17:34.157 --> 00:17:36.709
you need to focus on headings, right?

00:17:36.709 --> 00:17:38.376
So you see headings.

00:17:39.756 --> 00:17:41.188
Within your blog posts,

00:17:41.188 --> 00:17:43.725
you need to use them three to five times.

00:17:43.725 --> 00:17:46.225
This will help you break it up.

00:17:46.225 --> 00:17:47.664
It keeps it simple, right?

00:17:47.664 --> 00:17:50.950
How many times have you guys
signed up for a service?

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:53.234
Once, twice, three times?

00:17:53.234 --> 00:17:54.620
I bet all of you guys have signed up

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:58.008
for more than 50 services in your lifetime

00:17:58.008 --> 00:18:00.325
in which you're putting in
your name and email somewhere.

00:18:00.325 --> 00:18:01.750
Would you agree with that?

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:03.736
Probably even 100, right?

00:18:03.736 --> 00:18:04.865
What happens if I could tell you

00:18:04.865 --> 00:18:06.709
that you can sign up for a product

00:18:06.709 --> 00:18:08.991
without giving your name,
email, and password?

00:18:08.991 --> 00:18:09.936
Would you be happy,

00:18:09.936 --> 00:18:12.585
assuming it doesn't
break any privacy laws,

00:18:12.585 --> 00:18:14.679
all your information's secure, et cetera?

00:18:14.679 --> 00:18:16.512
Would you be happy?

00:18:16.512 --> 00:18:17.429
Yes, right?

00:18:18.314 --> 00:18:20.167
Well, you can.

00:18:20.167 --> 00:18:21.360
So Buffer.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:23.463
Log in with Twitter, log in with Facebook,

00:18:23.463 --> 00:18:24.930
log in with LinkedIn,

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:27.143
or log in with an email address.

00:18:27.143 --> 00:18:28.620
A lot of people are doing it.

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:30.934
I understand, they're a
social media business,

00:18:30.934 --> 00:18:32.511
but anyone can do this.

00:18:32.511 --> 00:18:34.321
We do this at KISSmetrics.

00:18:34.321 --> 00:18:38.411
KISSmetrics sells B2B
solutions for businesses.

00:18:38.411 --> 00:18:39.878
So you would think, oh,

00:18:39.878 --> 00:18:42.124
people would use their
corporate email address,

00:18:42.124 --> 00:18:44.143
you know, Facebook wouldn't work,

00:18:44.143 --> 00:18:45.561
especially Gmail, right?

00:18:45.561 --> 00:18:47.068
You guys wouldn't think Gmail would work

00:18:47.068 --> 00:18:49.732
for a business that only
sells to other businesses?

00:18:49.732 --> 00:18:51.729
Well, we decided to test it out.

00:18:51.729 --> 00:18:53.215
Sign up with your Google account.

00:18:53.215 --> 00:18:54.812
We didn't even give another option

00:18:54.812 --> 00:18:57.623
that stated also sign up with your email.

00:18:57.623 --> 00:19:00.785
We made you sign up with
your Google account, right?

00:19:00.785 --> 00:19:02.783
Just that one little change

00:19:02.783 --> 00:19:05.450
increased signups by 59.4%.

00:19:07.245 --> 00:19:09.003
Then we decided to do something funny.

00:19:09.003 --> 00:19:10.793
I can see the Unbound
person shaking their head.

00:19:10.793 --> 00:19:13.432
You're going to try this, aren't ya?

00:19:13.432 --> 00:19:14.865
All right, try this after.

00:19:14.865 --> 00:19:15.929
It even worked better.

00:19:15.929 --> 00:19:17.271
So we decided to do something else.

00:19:17.271 --> 00:19:18.726
We're like, you know what?

00:19:18.726 --> 00:19:20.578
I hate signing up.

00:19:20.578 --> 00:19:21.789
I don't mind logging into sites,

00:19:21.789 --> 00:19:23.819
but I don't like signing up.

00:19:23.819 --> 00:19:25.955
So we decided to change
a call to action button

00:19:25.955 --> 00:19:28.705
to say log in with Google, right?

00:19:29.541 --> 00:19:33.120
We got another 22% increase in signups

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:37.252
just by changing the word
from sign up to log in.

00:19:37.252 --> 00:19:39.059
So if you take both the convertor rates,

00:19:39.059 --> 00:19:42.089
the 59 and the 22, it compounds, right?

00:19:42.089 --> 00:19:43.932
You're looking at roughly, like, a 90-plus

00:19:43.932 --> 00:19:45.920
percent increase in conversions.

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:48.252
That's not too shabby.

00:19:48.252 --> 00:19:50.484
All by just using Google.

00:19:50.484 --> 00:19:54.302
And we have customers like
Microsoft, Amazon, et cetera.

00:19:54.302 --> 00:19:57.808
They all signed up
through the Google funnel.

00:19:57.808 --> 00:20:01.189
And you would think people at
Microsoft aren't using Gmail.

00:20:01.189 --> 00:20:04.807
I can prove to you they are, right?

00:20:04.807 --> 00:20:06.170
So if someone's telling you,

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:07.043
oh, it's not going to work,

00:20:07.043 --> 00:20:08.757
it's not what we're testing,

00:20:08.757 --> 00:20:10.176
you know what, try it out.

00:20:10.176 --> 00:20:11.302
You can't go wrong.

00:20:11.302 --> 00:20:13.697
Just send 10% of your traffic.

00:20:13.697 --> 00:20:15.644
What's the worst that's going to happen?

